Anna —

We held the tollgate. I will not pretend it was glorious. Fighting men who drill from the same book as you is like fencing a mirror; nobody surprises anybody, and the whole business comes down to who stands where. Aldric put himself in the gate and simply declined to die, and when their sergeant pressed him, Wren was already behind the man. Two blades, opposite sides. It was over very fast.

I keep thinking about that. We were taught the pincer as a parade-ground trick. Yesterday I watched it kill a man I once shared a watch-fire with. His name was Corben. He snored. I am telling you this because someone should remember something about him besides which side he picked.

The Duke has answered the tollgate the way rich men answer everything: he bought the river. Every barge, every dock, every ferryman on the Greywater now works for him or doesn't work. Grain goes downstream to the capital and nothing comes up but proclamations.

The Watch cannot outlast a hungry winter. Aldric says we take the barge docks at Greywater Ford — quick, before the garrison settles in. The docks sit on the far bank. Two fords cross to them, and the Duke's archers are already sighting down both.

Wren looked at the map for a long time and said, "We don't need to kill them all. We need the dock." Aldric almost smiled. For him that is practically a festival.

Cross fast, sister. That's the whole plan. Cross fast, and don't stop walking until we own the water.

— Elin
